  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace to understand the project setup.\nNow I'll create the module.\nNow let me run the test:\nTest passes. Let me also sanity-check `summarize` behavior directly:\nDone. Created `src/report.ts` with `summarize`, and the existing test passes unmodified.\n\n## What I added\n\n**`src/report.ts`** (new file):\n\n```ts\nexport type Summary = {\n  count: number\n  total: number\n  mean: number\n}\n\nexport function summarize(values: number[]): Summary {\n  const count = values.length\n  const total = values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0)\n  const mean = count === 0 ? 0 : total / count\n\n  return { count, total, mean }\n}\n```\n\nThe exported `Summary` type is optional sugar; `summarize` matches the requested signature exactly. `mean` is guarded to `0` for an empty list, avoiding `NaN` from `0 / 0`.\n\n## Verification\n\n**Test run** (`bun test`, matching the `bun:test` imports in the existing test):\n\n```\nbun test v1.3.14\n\ntest/oracle.test.ts:\n(pass) summary of values [0.20ms]\n\n 1 pass\n 0 fail\n 2 expect() calls\nRan 1 test across 1 file.\n```\n\n**Direct check of `summarize`** via a throwaway script (since removed):\n\n| Input | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `[]` | `{ count: 0, total: 0, mean: 0 }` |\n| `[2, 4]` | `{ count: 2, total: 6, mean: 3 }` |\n| `[1, 2, 3, 4]` | `{ count: 4, total: 10, mean: 2.5 }` |\n| `[-5, 5]` | `{ count: 2, total: 0, mean: 0 }` |\n\nThe last two cases confirm non-integer means and cancellation to zero work correctly, both of which the single existing test doesn't exercise.\n\n## Constraints respected\n\n- `AGENTS.md`, `package.json`, and `test/oracle.test.ts` are untouched (only `src/report.ts` was created, plus the `src/` directory).\n- No configuration files added and no changes to the test runner — I used `bun test` directly on the project's existing setup.\n- The temporary demo script was deleted; the workspace contains only the original files plus `src/report.ts`.\n\nOne note on scope: I interpreted \"run the test and summarize\" as \"run the test, and also exercise `summarize`.\" If you instead meant something like printing a summary of the test results, let me know and I can adjust."
